Motion Installation Guide
=========================

PLATFORM REQUIREMENTS
---------------------
Ubuntu / Debian (64-bit)
    Motion builds on Ubuntu 22.04+ and Debian 12+ (amd64 or arm64).
    FFmpeg 5.0 or newer is required; Ubuntu 22.04 and Debian 12 both
    provide a compatible version from their default repositories.
    Any system with the required libraries and a supported camera
    interface (V4L2 or libcamera) should work.

Raspberry Pi (64-bit)
    Raspberry Pi 4 or newer running Raspberry Pi OS Bookworm or newer (64-bit, arm64). Raspberry Pi 3 and older are NOT supported. Pi 5 requires libcamera; the legacy V4L2 camera stack was removed from Pi 5 Raspberry Pi OS images.

DEBIAN / UBUNTU INSTALLATION
----------------------------
The packages and library names change occasionally across operating
system versions. Adjust the following as needed for your OS.

Install required packages:
    sudo apt install autoconf automake autopoint build-essential pkgconf \
        libtool gettext git \
        libjpeg-dev zlib1g-dev libmicrohttpd-dev libxcrypt-dev \
        libavformat-dev libavcodec-dev libavutil-dev libswscale-dev libavdevice-dev

Install optional but recommended packages:
    sudo apt install libsqlite3-dev libwebp-dev libsystemd-dev

For libcamera support (required on Pi 5, recommended on Pi 4):
    sudo apt install libcamera-dev libcamera-tools libcamera-v4l2

For frontend development (optional — rebuilds the React web UI):
    curl -fsSL https://deb.nodesource.com/setup_20.x | sudo -E bash -
    sudo apt install nodejs

Once required packages are installed, execute:
    autoreconf -fiv
    ./configure --with-sqlite3
    make
    sudo make install

RASPBERRY PI INSTALLATION (Recommended)
---------------------------------------
For Raspberry Pi 4/5, use the automated installation script.
Clone or copy the source to the Pi, then from the source directory run:

    # Run the automated installer (interactive)
    sudo scripts/install.sh

    # Access the web UI
    http://<pi-ip>:8080/

The installer will prompt for admin and viewer passwords during setup.

UNATTENDED INSTALLATION
-----------------------
For automated deployments (e.g., provisioning scripts, Ansible):

    sudo scripts/install.sh --unattended \
        --admin-pass "your-admin-password" \
        --viewer-pass "your-viewer-password"

Additional options:
    --viewer-user USER   Set viewer username (default: viewer)
    --skip-service       Skip systemd service setup
    --help               Show all options

MANUAL INSTALLATION (Step by Step)
----------------------------------
If you prefer to run each step manually:

    # 1. Install dependencies
    sudo scripts/pi-setup.sh

    # 2. Build Motion (backend + frontend)
    scripts/pi-build.sh

    # 3. Install system-wide (binaries, webui, service file)
    sudo make install
    sudo make install-service

    # 4. Configure authentication (required)
    sudo motion-setup

    # 5. Set up and start the systemd service
    sudo systemctl enable motion
    sudo systemctl start motion

    # Access the web UI
    http://<pi-ip>:8080/

The pi-setup.sh script installs:
- Build tools (autoconf, automake, g++, etc.)
- Camera support (libcamera for Pi camera modules)
- Video encoding (FFmpeg 5.0+)
- Web server (libmicrohttpd)
- Database (SQLite3)
- Node.js (for frontend build)

AUTHENTICATION SETUP
--------------------
After installation, configure authentication credentials:

    sudo motion-setup

This creates admin and viewer accounts with bcrypt-hashed passwords.
Run with --reset to update passwords on an existing installation.

CONFIGURE OPTIONS
-----------------
    --prefix=DIR             Install to DIR instead of /usr/local
    --with-libcam[=DIR]      Enable libcamera support (auto-detected;
                             required on Pi 5, recommended on Pi 4)
    --without-v4l2           Disable V4L2 support (use when only
                             libcamera cameras are present)
    --with-sqlite3           Enable SQLite3 event database support
    --with-webp              Enable WebP image output support
    --with-webrtc            Enable WebRTC streaming via libdatachannel
                             (requires libdatachannel to be built separately)
    --with-opencv[=DIR]      Enable OpenCV support
    --with-mariadb[=DIR]     Enable MariaDB database support
    --with-mysql[=DIR]       Enable MySQL database support
    --with-pgsql[=DIR]       Enable PostgreSQL database support
    --with-ffmpeg[=DIR]      Specify a non-system FFmpeg location (FFmpeg
                             5.0+ is required and cannot be disabled)
    --without-systemd        Disable systemd watchdog integration
    --with-developer-flags   Enable strict compiler warnings (for development)
    --disable-hardening      Disable compiler security hardening flags
